jobs:
  example-job:
    steps:
      - name: Cache node modules
        uses: actions/cache@v3
        env:
          cache-name: cache-node-modules
        with:
          path: ~/.npm
          key: ${{ runner.os }}-build-${{ env.cache-name }}-${{ hashFiles('**/package-lock.json') }}
          restore-keys: |
            ${{ runner.os }}-build-${{ env.cache-name }}-


1. Action Used: actions/cache@v3
The configuration uses the actions/cache action, which is a built-in GitHub Action designed to cache files and directories between workflow runs.
2. Cache Key:
The key for the cache is defined as ${{ runner.os }}-build-${{ env.cache-name }}-${{ hashFiles('**/package-lock.json') }}.
runner.os is the operating system of the runner (e.g., ubuntu-latest, windows-latest).
cache-name is a custom environment variable (cache-node-modules) that can be used for descriptive purposes.
hashFiles('**/package-lock.json') generates a hash based on the contents of all package-lock.json files in the repository. 
This ensures that if the dependencies (e.g., in package-lock.json) change, the cache key will change, forcing the cache to be refreshed.
3. Cache Path:
The path specified is ~/.npm, which points to the directory where Node.js stores its npm modules locally (i.e., the cache for node modules). 
This means the cached data will include the installed npm modules that were previously saved.
4. Restore Keys:
restore-keys is an optional list of cache keys to try when looking for an existing cache. If the exact cache key is not found, 
GitHub Actions will attempt to restore from keys with a common prefix. In this case, the restore key is defined as ${{ runner.os }}-build-${{ env.cache-name }}-, 
which allows matching any cache with the same prefix, but not necessarily the same hash of package-lock.json. This helps recover some cached data even if the 
dependencies have changed slightly.
Workflow:
Cache Lookup: When the workflow runs, it checks if a cache exists for the given key 
(${{ runner.os }}-build-${{ env.cache-name }}-${{ hashFiles('**/package-lock.json') }}).
If a matching cache is found, it is restored, and the cached npm modules are available.
If no cache is found, npm dependencies will need to be reinstalled, and a new cache will be created after the npm install step completes.
Cache Creation: After installing the npm dependencies, the cache is saved with the computed key for future use.
This caching mechanism helps reduce the time spent reinstalling dependencies on each run of the workflow, especially when the dependencies haven’t changed.
